<p id="p10">Rather than rehash what I already <a href="https://www.macstories.net/club/macstories-weekly-issue-537/introducing-macremote-bridge-your-local-macs-tools-and-mcps-to-any-ai-agent/" rel="noopener noreferrer">wrote</a>, here’s how I described MacRemote to Club members during the beta process:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote11"><p>
  The idea behind MacRemote was born out of frustration: I wanted to use products like Claude Cowork or ChatGPT Work – and, later, the likes of Muse or Grok Bot – but those services were running in their own isolated environments and had no access to my Mac Studio. And my Mac Studio had plenty of tools I wanted to be able to access: from my advanced <a href="https://github.com/viticci/remctl" rel="noopener noreferrer">Reminders</a> or <a href="https://github.com/viticci/frames-cli" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apple Frames CLIs</a> to local MCP servers that were running exclusively on my Mac, I didn’t want to give up on the apps and tools I was using locally on my Mac only because those other agents were running in the cloud. There were some options, such as <a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/secure-mcp-tunnels" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenAI’s Secure MCP Tunnels</a> for local MCP servers, but they were too fiddly to set up, did not support generic command-line interfaces, and they were specific to one company. Instead, I wanted a secure, all-in-one, permission-based application with a polished macOS UI that would let me intuitively choose which parts of my Mac I wanted to expose to another AI running remotely. Thus MacRemote was born.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p12">And:</p>
<p id="p13">I’ve been running my own custom domain in MacRemote for weeks at a time without ever rebooting my Mac or stopping the tunnel; it’s worked amazingly well. If you’re unsure about how to set up a Cloudflare domain and required token to deploy it in MacRemote, just ask your agent to read the docs and help you in the process.<br>
The other aspect of MacRemote I’m especially proud of is the automatic discovery of CLIs and MCP servers already installed on your Mac. I started from the assumption that, if I have a Mac that is always on, available, <em>and</em> under my control, I shouldn’t have to replicate my setup in a cloud environment for a new agent every single time. <strong>My Mac is the source of truth and has all my files and tools I want to use.</strong> MacRemote is able to automatically detect CLIs installed on your machine, categorize them by type and source, and it can also offer to import MCP servers you’ve already configured in Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, or other desktop agents. For each MCP server, MacRemote extracts individual tools, understands which ones are safer than others and which ones perform potentially destructive actions, and lets you choose which tools you want to make available remotely.</p>
<p id="p14">While every cloud-based agent these days is shipping its own version of macOS integration, thus resulting in a fragmented ecosystem of how agents can reach out to your Mac, MacRemote is an agent-agnostic solution that makes any Mac tool or local MCP server you want available, with extensive permissions, to multiple agents at the same time.</p>
<p id="p15">Want to run your local <code>ffmpeg</code> setup from Claude’s website or Claude Duet? That works. You like Muse, but wish it could use RemCTL or Xcode’s MCP server? That can be done. You use Dots, Grok Bot, Hermes, or an agentic app for iOS and wish they could grab files from your Mac, but not delete them, and also do something in Python with them? How about accessing local AI models, remotely? Or perhaps you’d like ChatGPT Work to kick off a Computer Use agent like the amazing <a href="https://cua.ai/cua-driver" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cua Driver</a> on your own Mac mini, rather than some cloud sandbox? Yes, yes, and yes. MacRemote can do it all.</p>
