<p id="p21">I strongly believe in the idea of <strong>owning your compute</strong> and having full control over the tools that you want to make available to agents. And while – rightfully so, I’d say – each AI lab is shipping its own (limited) version of macOS integration, MacRemote provides you with a centralized, Mac-native way to select the tools you want, browse a catalog of CLIs and MCPs found on your computer, and decide exactly what you want to do with them.</p>
<p id="p22">Like my other open-source projects, MacRemote has been a labor of love and helped me learn a lot about a new field in the process. Feature and pull requests are welcome, and I urge you – if you’re so inclined – to look at the source code and tell me if it should be better in any particular way. Since I’m the first user of MacRemote, it’s in my own interest to maintain it and grow it alongside the MCP spec for the foreseeable future. I expect nothing in return: I’d just love for you to try it and tell me how it could be improved.</p>
<p id="p23"><a href="https://github.com/viticci/MacRemote" rel="noopener noreferrer">MacRemote is free, open source under an MIT license</a>, and it’s become an essential tool in my agentic workflow. I hope you’ll like it as much as I loved building it.</p>
